# LIVE CONTROL — the CALLEE-DISPOSITION axis: the callee MOVES ITS `!` PARAM
# ONWARD (here, returns it).
#
# Green at HEAD and must STAY green. This is a guard against a specific,
# measured hazard rather than a repro of a live bug.
#
# THE HAZARD IT GUARDS. `lower_call_arg`'s clone for a `MoveParamFromBorrow`
# arg does NOT register the clone for drops. Routing that site through
# `ensure_owned_at_consuming_arg` — whose Case 1 DOES `register_local` — adds a
# caller-side drop with no compensating `MoveZero`/mark. Measured under ASan:
# with that routing simulated, this program aborts with
# `AddressSanitizer: attempting double-free`; at HEAD it prints `4 / done`.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT AN EASIER ONE. `drop_if_alive` is self-nulling, so
# when the callee simply DROPS its `!` param the caller's extra drop is a
# harmless no-op — which is why the one pre-existing fixture over this site
# stayed green under the same change. The bug is only observable when the
# callee moves the value ONWARD, so the callee-disposition axis (callee drops ·
# callee returns it · callee stashes it in a longer-lived container) is the
# axis that matters, and it had NO coverage before these two fixtures.
#
# Run under `--sanitize`; a plain run would not catch it.

from std.collections import Vector

Vector[int] take_and_return(Vector[int] !v):
    v.push(99)
    return !v

void main():
    Vector[Vector[int]] matrix = Vector[Vector[int]]()
    Vector[int] row1 = [1, 2, 3]
    matrix.push(!row1)
    auto ref_row = matrix[0]
    Vector[int] got = take_and_return(!ref_row)
    print(got.len())
    print("done")
